[TITLE] Autonomous monitoring and maintenance of hybrid power stations in remote areas
[P1] A plateau test station relies on solar arrays, lithium batteries and a small diesel generator to power it. Micro-grids need to continue to support clinics, radio relay, weather equipment and safety lighting. Roads can be closed for days in winter and the network is unstable, so local controllers collect voltage, current, temperature and vibration data every 200 milliseconds, and prioritize medical and communications loads when energy is scarce. The complete record is stored on the station, and only the compressed status summary is sent to the control center when the link is restored.
[P2] On October 18, 2025, a dust storm caused a 34 percent drop in solar output in fifteen minutes, a temperature rise of eight degrees Celsius in the battery cabinet, and an abnormally low frequency vibration in the inverter fan. Instead of shutting down the entire power plant, the controller disconnects unnecessary heating devices, lowers the power of some lighting and starts the backup generator. Diagnostic modules then suggested three possible reasons for the shredded solar panels, fan bearing wear, and increased contact resistance of the battery connector.
[P3] After the storm weakened, technicians used head-mounted cameras to read the insignia, serial numbers and alarm codes, and the local processing unit was compared to the offline maintenance manual. The images were not uploaded to the cloud because the material involved critical infrastructure. The capture process first confirms that the entire label is within the safety boundary and checks the clarity of the whole image and the upper area separately: if the image is cropped, the headset prompts to move left, right or backwards. The target is saved only when the sequence of four lines is stable and the text is clear enough. The next day's logs showed that the automatic sorting was consistent with the artificial judgment: first the panels were cleaned, then the fan bearings were replaced, and finally the battery terminals were tightened. The disposal took six minutes and critical services were not interrupted. This shows that reliable automation must interpret judgments, acknowledge uncertainty, and leave the final decision to humans.
